The '''2015 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League]], the 60th season of Europe's premier club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 23rd season since it was renamed from the European Champion Clubs' Cup to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was played at the [[Olympiastadion (Berlin)|Olympiastadion]] in [[Berlin]], Germany, on 6 June 2015, between Italian side [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] and Spanish side [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]].

For the second time – after [[2010 UEFA Champions League final|2010]] – both teams came into the final with the possibility of definitively winning a [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]], having each won their national league and cup for the season. Barcelona scored the only goal of the first half after four minutes, through [[Ivan Rakitić]]. Ten minutes after the interval, Juventus equalised with a goal by [[Álvaro Morata]]. In the 68th minute, [[Luis Suárez (Uruguayan footballer)|Luis Suárez]] put Barcelona back in the lead, and Barcelona's win was confirmed when [[Neymar]] scored with the last kick of the game. It was Barcelona's fifth trophy in the competition, and sealed their second treble, the other coming in [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009]]. It was also Juventus' sixth defeat in a European Cup final, the most by any club, a record they previously shared with [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] and [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]].

As winners, Barcelona earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2014–15 UEFA Europa League]], [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], in the [[2015 UEFA Super Cup]], and won that match 5–4. They also qualified to enter the [[2015 FIFA Club World Cup]] in Japan as the UEFA representative, going on to beat Argentina's [[CA River Plate|River Plate]] in the [[2015 FIFA Club World Cup final|final]].

==Venue==
[[File:Olympicstadium2.jpg|thumb|The Olympiastadion in Berlin hosted the final.]]

The [[Olympiastadion (Berlin)|Olympiastadion]] was announced as the venue for the final at the UEFA Executive Committee meeting in London on 23 May 2013.<ref>{{cite web |title=Executive Committee decides hosts for 2015 finals |url=http://www.uefa.org/about-uefa/executive-committee/news/newsid=1954884.html |website=UEFA.org |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=23 May 2013 |access-date=14 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151004072345/http://www.uefa.org/about-uefa/executive-committee/news/newsid=1954884.html |archive-date=4 October 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref> This was the first European Cup/Champions League final hosted in Berlin.<ref>{{cite news|last=with wires|first=kla|title=Berlin to Host 2015 Final in Olympic Stadium|url=http://www.spiegel.de/international/germany/2015-champions-league-final-to-be-held-in-berlin-germany-a-901526.html|access-date=9 August 2016|newspaper=[[Der Spiegel]]|date=23 May 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160414052521/http://www.spiegel.de/international/germany/2015-champions-league-final-to-be-held-in-berlin-germany-a-901526.html|archive-date=14 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

The current Olympiastadion was built for the [[1936 Summer Olympics]] in the western part of the city.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.worldsoccer.com/features/stadium-guide/stadium-guide-olympiastadion-berlin-362518|title=Stadium Guide: Olympiastadion Berlin - World Soccer|publisher=WorldSoccer.com|date=5 June 2015|access-date=9 August 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160827105131/http://www.worldsoccer.com/features/stadium-guide/stadium-guide-olympiastadion-berlin-362518|archive-date=27 August 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Since 1985, the stadium has hosted the finals of both the [[DFB-Pokal]] and its [[DFB-Pokal (women)|female equivalent]]. The Olympiastadion hosts the [[ISTAF Berlin|Internationales Stadionfest]], which was an [[IAAF Golden League]] event from 1998 to 2009. The stadium hosted the [[2009 World Championships in Athletics]].<ref name=Host1>{{cite web |title=Berlin to host 2009 World Championships |url=http://www.iaaf.org/news/Kind=512/newsId=27957.html |publisher=[[IAAF]] |date=4 April 2004 |access-date=9 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121022192316/http://www.iaaf.org/news/Kind=512/newsId=27957.html |archive-date=22 October 2012}}</ref>

Aside from its use as an Olympic stadium, the Olympiastadion has a strong footballing tradition, having been the home of [[Hertha BSC]] since 1963.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.herthabsc.de/de/teams/damals-wars-nuernberg/page/4337--45--.html#.V6ovHFQrK00|title=DAMALS WAR'S: BUNDESLIGA-AUFTAKT GEGEN DEN 'CLUB'|date=24 August 2015 |publisher=herthabsc.de|access-date=9 August 2016|language=de|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160821093238/http://www.herthabsc.de/de/teams/damals-wars-nuernberg/page/4337--45--.html#.V6ovHFQrK00|archive-date=21 August 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> It was also used for three matches at the [[1974 FIFA World Cup]], and was renovated ahead of the [[2006 FIFA World Cup|2006 tournament]], at which it hosted six matches, including the [[2006 FIFA World Cup final|final]].<ref name="report">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=97410600/match=97410064/index.html#nosticky |title=Italy – France |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |access-date=9 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160811075340/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=97410600/match=97410064/index.html#nosticky |archive-date=11 August 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Background==
This was the eighth European Cup/UEFA Champions League final for both Juventus and Barcelona.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0221-0e9b62f81668-026e01bbda09-1000--juventus-and-barcelona-the-final-victories/|title=Juventus and Barcelona: the final victories|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=31 May 2015|access-date=31 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160109213333/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2250446.html|archive-date=9 January 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Juventus won two of their previous finals ([[1985 European Cup final|1985]], [[1996 UEFA Champions League final|1996]]) and lost five ([[1973 European Cup final|1973]], [[1983 European Cup final|1983]], [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|1997]], [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]], [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]]), while Barcelona won four of their previous finals ([[1992 European Cup final|1992]], [[2006 UEFA Champions League final|2006]], [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009]], [[2011 UEFA Champions League final|2011]]) and lost three ([[1961 European Cup final|1961]], [[1986 European Cup final|1986]], [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]]). Barcelona also played in six [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (winning in [[1979 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1979]], [[1982 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1982]], [[1989 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1989]], [[1997 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup final|1997]], and losing in [[1969 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1969]], [[1991 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1991]]), while Juventus also played in one Cup Winners' Cup final (winning in [[1984 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1984]]) and four [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (winning in [[1977 UEFA Cup final|1977]], [[1990 UEFA Cup final|1990]], [[1993 UEFA Cup final|1993]], and losing in [[1995 UEFA Cup final|1995]]).<ref>{{cite web |title=2015 UEFA Champions League Final Press Kit |url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2015/E/E_2015227_PK.pdf |work=[[UEFA]] |date=5 June 2015 |access-date=5 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151208093042/http://www.uefa.org/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2015/E/E_2015227_PK.pdf |archive-date=8 December 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The two teams had previously played six times in UEFA club competitions, but never in a final. In their previous UEFA club competition meetings, Barcelona won 2–1 on aggregate in the [[1985–86 European Cup]] quarter-finals and 3–2 on aggregate in the [[1990–91 European Cup Winners' Cup]] semi-finals, while Juventus won 3–2 on aggregate in the [[2002–03 UEFA Champions League]] quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Juventus v Barcelona: past meetings |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0221-0e90b42c667a-601278464a05-1000--juventus-v-barcelona-past-meetings/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=13 May 2015 |access-date=26 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150526024715/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2246724.html |archive-date=26 May 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref> They also played in the 1952 [[Latin Cup]] semi-finals, won by Barcelona 4–2, and the [[1970–71 Inter-Cities Fairs Cup]] second round, won by Juventus 4–2 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/world-match-centre/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid/261/901/4/index.html|title=Barça-Juve: Five battles in three cities|publisher=FIFA.com|date=5 June 2015|access-date=4 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305151914/http://www.fifa.com/world-match-centre/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid/261/901/4/index.html|archive-date=5 March 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Similar to the [[2010 UEFA Champions League final]], both teams entered the final in the possibility of winning the [[treble (association football)|treble]] of domestic league, domestic cup and Champions League titles.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d801f260f58-ddd88205fd28-1000--barcelona-break-new-ground-with-second-treble/|title=Barcelona and Juventus go for treble glory|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=31 May 2015|access-date=30 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171119115620/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2251733.html|archive-date=19 November 2017|url-status=live}}</ref> Juventus were crowned champions of the [[2014–15 Serie A]] on 2 May, and won the [[2015 Coppa Italia final]] eighteen days later. Barcelona were crowned champions of the [[2014–15 La Liga]] on 17 May, and won the [[2015 Copa del Rey final]] thirteen days later. While it would have been the first treble for Juventus, Barcelona had previously won the treble in [[2008–09 FC Barcelona season|2008–09]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Barcelona 2–0 Man Utd |work=BBC Sport |date=27 May 2009 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8060878.stm |access-date=27 September 2020}}</ref>

