===Juventus===
[[File:Carlos_T%C3%A9vez,_Real_Madrid_vs_Juventus,_24_October_2013_Champions_League.JPG|thumb|upright|[[Carlos Tevez]] scored seven goals in Juventus' run to the final.]]

As winners of the [[2013–14 Serie A]], Juventus qualified automatically for the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League group stage|group stage]]. They were seeded second in Group A featuring – in seeded order – [[Atlético Madrid]] (champions of the [[2013–14 La Liga]]), [[Olympiacos F.C.|Olympiacos]] (champions of the [[2013–14 Super League Greece]]), and [[Malmö FF]] (champions of the [[2013 Allsvenskan]]).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/clubs/50139--juventus/|title=Juventus Matches|access-date=9 August 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160816094231/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/clubs/club=50139/matches/index.html|archive-date=16 August 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

Their campaign began on 16 September, hosting Malmö at the [[Juventus Stadium]], winning 2–0 through second-half goals by [[Carlos Tevez]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Tévez gets Juventus going at Malmö's expense|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014316--juventus-vs-malmo/|access-date=11 April 2016|date=16 September 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161211121604/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014316/index.html|archive-date=11 December 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> On 1 October, they travelled to face Atlético at the [[Vicente Calderón Stadium]], losing by a sole goal from [[Arda Turan]] late on, a result that left all four teams level on three points apiece.<ref>{{cite news|title=Arda Turan edges Atlético past Juventus|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014324--atleti-vs-juventus/|access-date=11 April 2016|date=1 October 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160310124533/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014324/index.html|archive-date=10 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Three weeks later Juventus again lost by one goal on their travels, [[Pajtim Kasami]] scoring in the first half for Olympiacos, whose goalkeeper [[Roberto Jiménez Gago|Roberto]] played a key role.<ref>{{cite news|title=Roberto denies Juventus as Olympiacos win again|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014340--olympiacos-vs-juventus/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=22 October 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151212005749/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014340/index.html|archive-date=12 December 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> On 4 November, hosting the Greeks, Juventus won 3–2 after trailing 1–2, getting two goals in as many minutes from Roberto's [[own goal]] and [[Paul Pogba]]'s winning strike.<ref>{{cite news|title=Pogba caps Juventus recovery against Olympiacos|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014364--juventus-vs-olympiacos/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=4 November 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160310200059/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014364/index.html|archive-date=10 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 26 November, Juventus won away for the first time in the group, with second-half goals from [[Fernando Llorente]] and Tevez at Malmö's [[Swedbank Stadion]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Juventus end Malmö bid to close in on qualification|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014372--malmo-vs-juventus/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=26 November 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150920210855/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014372/index.html|archive-date=20 September 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> In the final group game on 9 December, they hosted a goalless draw against Atlético, which sent both teams through, but Juventus in second place.<ref>{{cite news|title=Honours even for Juventus and Atlético|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014396--juventus-vs-atleti/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=9 December 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160310200244/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014396/index.html|archive-date=10 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

As group runners-up, Juventus had to face a group winner – Germans [[Borussia Dortmund]] of Group D – in the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League knockout phase|last 16]]. They won 2–1 at home in the first leg on 24 February 2015, with all the goals in the first half – visitor [[Marco Reus]] equalised either side of strikes from Tevez and [[Álvaro Morata]].<ref>{{cite news|title=First-half goals give Juve victory over Dortmund|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014408--juventus-vs-b-dortmund/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=24 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160319034522/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000549/match=2014408/index.html|archive-date=19 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> The same forwards were on target in the second leg at the [[Westfalenstadion]] on 18 March – Tevez scored twice, including in the third minute – as Juventus won 3–0, and 5–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Tévez [''sic''] leads Juventus to Dortmund stroll|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014418--b-dortmund-vs-juventus/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=18 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160316145411/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000549/match=2014418/index.html|archive-date=16 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, Juventus faced [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]], runners-up in the [[2013–14 Ligue 1]]. The only goal of the tie came in the first leg at home on 14 April, [[Arturo Vidal]] scoring a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]] after [[Ricardo Carvalho]] tripped Morata.<ref>{{cite news|title=Vidal gives Juventus the edge over Monaco|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0220-0e9b0cca7a9f-371ccb320070-1000--vidal-gives-juventus-the-edge-over-monaco/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=14 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160817054619/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000550/match=2014422/postmatch/report/index.html#juve+gain+narrow+lead|archive-date=17 August 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> It was their first advancement into the semi-finals for 12 years.<ref>{{cite news|title=Juventus edge past Monaco and into semis|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014424--monaco-vs-juventus/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=22 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160316145926/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000550/match=2014424/index.html|archive-date=16 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 5 May, Juventus hosted reigning champions [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] at Juventus Stadium. Morata opened the scoring against his former team, and [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] equalised before half time. Tevez scored the winning goal from the penalty spot after he was fouled by [[Dani Carvajal]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Tévez [''sic''] gives Juventus the edge against Real Madrid|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0221-0e908d4a6138-0d200bf0b0d9-1000--tevez-gives-juventus-the-edge-against-real-madrid/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=5 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160105180434/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000551/match=2014427/postmatch/report/index.html#tevez+gives+juve+edge|archive-date=5 January 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Eight days later, the two teams drew at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium]]: Ronaldo scored a penalty after [[Giorgio Chiellini]] fouled [[James Rodríguez]], but in the second half Morata equalised. Juventus advanced to the final 3–2 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Morata takes Juve through at Madrid's expense|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0221-0e90b42c4d27-040295451f90-1000--morata-takes-juve-through-at-madrid-s-expense/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=13 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160106194158/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000551/match=2014430/postmatch/report/index.html#juve+oust+holders+madrid|archive-date=6 January 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Barcelona===
[[File:2015_UEFA_Super_Cup_64_crop.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Lionel Messi]] scored ten times in Barcelona's progress to the final, including a hat-trick away to [[APOEL FC|APOEL]].]]

Barcelona qualified for the group stage as runners-up to Atlético in the previous season's La Liga. They were the top seeds in Group F, alongside – in order – [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]] (winners of the [[2013–14 Ligue 1]]), [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] (winners of the [[2013–14 Eredivisie]]) and [[APOEL FC|APOEL]] (winners of the [[2013–14 Cypriot First Division]]).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/clubs/50080--barcelona/|title=Barcelona Matches|access-date=9 August 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160727080335/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/clubs/club=50080/matches/index.html|archive-date=27 July 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

In their first game of the group, defender [[Gerard Piqué]] headed the only goal to defeat [[APOEL]] at the [[Camp Nou]] on 17 September.<ref>{{cite news|title=Piqué the head boy as Barça edge past APOEL|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014309--barcelona-vs-apoel/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=17 September 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150426171257/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014309/index.html|archive-date=26 April 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> However, thirteen days later at the [[Parc des Princes]], they fell 3–2 to Paris Saint-Germain.<ref>{{cite news|title=Paris get better of Barcelona in five-goal thriller|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014333--paris-vs-barcelona/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=30 September 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160413125147/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014333/index.html|archive-date=13 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> On 21 October, Barcelona hosted Ajax and got first-half goals through [[Neymar]] and [[Lionel Messi]]; [[Anwar El Ghazi]] scored for the visitors with two minutes to play but [[Sandro Ramírez|Sandro]]'s added-time strike confirmed a 3–1 win.<ref>{{cite news|title=Irresistible Barça brush Ajax aside|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014350--barcelona-vs-ajax/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=21 October 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306095336/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014350/index.html|archive-date=6 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Barcelona travelled to the [[Amsterdam Arena]] on 5 November and defeated Ajax 2–0 with a Messi goal in each half; he equalled the tournament's record of 71 overall goals.<ref>{{cite news|title=Messi matches goal record as Barcelona advance|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014358--ajax-vs-barcelona/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=5 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150713081329/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014358/index.html|archive-date=13 July 2015|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 25 November, Barcelona played APOEL at the [[GSP Stadium]] in [[Nicosia]], winning 4–0 with a hat-trick from Messi and a further goal from [[Luis Suárez (Uruguayan footballer)|Luis Suárez]]; Messi became the competition's all-time top scorer.<ref>{{cite news|title=Record-breaker Messi leads Barça stroll at APOEL|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014381--apoel-vs-barcelona/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=25 November 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180616194701/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014381/index.html|archive-date=16 June 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> Barcelona won the group with a 3–1 home win over PSG on 10 December. They initially fell behind to a goal by [[Zlatan Ibrahimović]], but replied with a goal each from the South American forward line of Messi, Neymar and Suárez.<ref>{{cite news|title=Barcelona win Group F ahead of Paris|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014389--barcelona-vs-paris/|publisher=UEFA|date=10 December 2014|access-date=11 April 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160413100436/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000548/match=2014389/index.html|archive-date=13 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the last 16, Barcelona faced [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]], winners of the [[2013–14 Premier League]] and Group E runners-up. In the first leg at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]] on 24 February 2015, Suárez scored twice in the first 30 minutes, and [[Sergio Agüero]] got a goal back for the hosts after the break.<ref>{{cite news|title=Suárez at the double as Barcelona win at City|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014404--man-city-vs-barcelona/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=24 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160409100023/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000549/match=2014404/index.html|archive-date=9 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> In the return game on 18 March, City goalkeeper [[Joe Hart]] limited Barcelona to a single goal by [[Ivan Rakitić]], and Barcelona advanced 3–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Barcelona get past City despite Hart heroics|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014414--barcelona-vs-man-city/|access-date=11 April 2016|date=18 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160409100028/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000549/match=2014414/index.html|archive-date=9 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

Barcelona met PSG again in the quarter-finals. On 15 April in Paris, Neymar scored the only goal of the first half and Suárez added two more, with the hosts getting a consolation through [[Jérémy Mathieu]]'s own goal.<ref>{{cite news|title=Suárez at the double as Barcelona beat Paris|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014419--paris-vs-barcelona/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=15 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160414061633/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000550/match=2014419/index.html|archive-date=14 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Six days later, two first-half goals from Neymar sealed a 5–1 aggregate win.<ref>{{cite news|title=Neymar at the double as Barcelona ease through|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014425--barcelona-vs-paris/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=21 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160409092523/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000550/match=2014425/index.html|archive-date=9 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Barcelona's opponents were [[Bayern Munich]], winners of the [[2013–14 Bundesliga]]. They hosted the first leg on 6 May, breaking the deadlock with two late goals from Messi in a three-minute spell, and an added-time addition by Neymar.<ref>{{cite news|title=Messi double puts Barça in control against Bayern|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014428--barcelona-vs-bayern-munchen/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=6 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160408190522/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000551/match=2014428/index.html|archive-date=8 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Six days later at the [[Allianz Arena]], Bayern took the lead through [[Medhi Benatia]], and Neymar put Barcelona into the lead by half time. Second-half goals by [[Robert Lewandowski]] and [[Thomas Müller]] gave Bayern a 3–2 win on the night, but Barcelona advanced 5–3 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Barcelona hold on despite Bayern fightback|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2014429--bayern-munchen-vs-barcelona/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=12 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160413130013/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2015/matches/round=2000551/match=2014429/index.html|archive-date=13 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Pre-match==
===Ambassador===
[[File:Karl-Heinz Riedle, 2012.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Karl-Heinz Riedle]] was named as the ambassador for the final.]]

Former Germany international player [[Karl-Heinz Riedle]], who won the Champions League with [[Borussia Dortmund]] against Juventus in 1997, was named the ambassador for the final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Riedle relishing ambassadorial role |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d041e5b938f-e4aa01ce00f5-1000--riedle-relishing-ambassadorial-role/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=28 August 2014 |access-date=14 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150220034648/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2137889.html |archive-date=20 February 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Logo===
UEFA unveiled the visual identity of the final on 29 August 2014. It blends the stadium with the city's [[Brandenburg Gate]].<ref>{{cite web |title=2015 UEFA Champions League final identity revealed |url=http://www.uefa.org/about-uefa/administration/marketing/news/newsid=2137073.html |website=UEFA.org |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=29 August 2014 |access-date=14 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150419050245/http://www.uefa.org/about-uefa/administration/marketing/news/newsid=2137073.html |archive-date=19 April 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Ticketing===
With a stadium capacity of 70,500, a total amount of 46,000 tickets were available to fans and the general public, with the two finalist teams receiving 20,000 tickets each and with 6,000 tickets being available for sale to fans worldwide via UEFA.com from 5 to 23 March 2015 in four price categories: €390, €280, €160, and €70.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2217443.html|title=Apply for Champions League final tickets from Thursday|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=3 March 2015|access-date=17 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150313195751/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2217443.html|archive-date=13 March 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref> The rest of 24,500 tickets were allocated to sponsors and officials.<ref>{{cite news |title=Champions League final 2015: Uefa allocate 24,500 tickets for officials, sponsors and corporate hospitality |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/champions-league-final-2015-uefa-allocate-24500-tickets-for-officials-sponsors-and-corporate-hospitality-10083151.html |work=The Independent |publisher=Independent Print |date=3 March 2015 |access-date=17 March 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150329002907/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/champions-league-final-2015-uefa-allocate-24500-tickets-for-officials-sponsors-and-corporate-hospitality-10083151.html |archive-date=29 March 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Related events===
The [[2015 UEFA Women's Champions League final]] was held on 14 May 2015 at the [[Friedrich-Ludwig-Jahn-Sportpark]] in Berlin. Unlike recent years, in which the Women's Champions League final was held in the same week as the men's Champions League final, the two matches are separated by almost a month, as the [[2015 FIFA Women's World Cup]] started in early June.<ref>{{cite news |title=Friedrich-Ludwig-Jahn-Sportpark to stage final |url=https://www.uefa.com/womenschampionsleague/news/0217-0e159a8ddd93-e1c861fc2c67-1000--friedrich-ludwig-jahn-sportpark-to-stage-final/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 July 2014 |access-date=14 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150107052319/http://www.uefa.com/womenschampionsleague/news/newsid=2123817.html |archive-date=7 January 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The annual UEFA Champions Festival was held between 4–7 June 2015 in the streets around [[Brandenburg Gate]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0221-0e90c57d3d39-57766e18aea4-1000--champions-festival-berlin-4-7-june/|title=Champions Festival: Berlin, 4–7 June|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=18 May 2015|access-date=21 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150522002431/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2246749.html|archive-date=22 May 2015|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
The UEFA Champions League Anthem is performed by German soprano [[Nina Maria Fischer]] and Spanish tenor [[Manuel Gomez Ruiz]], the first time it had been performed as a duet.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.eldiario.es/canariasahora/sociedad/canario-cantara-Champions-final-sabado_0_395461204.html |title = Un tenor canario cantará el himno de la Champions en la final de este sábado  |date = 5 June 2015 |access-date=22 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190622153259/https://www.eldiario.es/canariasahora/sociedad/canario-cantara-Champions-final-sabado_0_395461204.html |archive-date=22 June 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==
===Officials===
[[File:Cüneyt Çakır.JPG|thumb|upright|[[Cüneyt Çakır]] was the final's referee.]]

In May 2015, the officials were chosen for the final by UEFA, led by Turkish referee [[Cüneyt Çakır]]. His compatriots Bahattin Duran and Tarık Ongun were chosen as assistant referees, and fellow Turks [[Hüseyin Göçek]] and Barış Şimşek the additional assistants, with Mustafa Emre Eyisoy the reserve assistant. [[Jonas Eriksson (referee)|Jonas Eriksson]], of Sweden, was chosen as fourth official.<ref>{{cite news|title=Turkish referee Cuneyt Cakir to take charge of Champions League final|url=http://www.espnfc.com/uefa-champions-league/story/2456059/cuneyt-cakir-to-referee-champions-league-final|access-date=6 June 2015|publisher=ESPN|date=18 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527164622/http://www.espnfc.com/uefa-champions-league/story/2456059/cuneyt-cakir-to-referee-champions-league-final|archive-date=27 May 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> Çakır is a UEFA elite referee, and has refereed at [[UEFA Euro 2012]] and the [[2014 FIFA World Cup]], as well as the [[2012 FIFA Club World Cup Final]].

Çakır was scrutinised by the British media in 2013 when he sent off [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]'s [[Nani (footballer)|Nani]] in a Champions League round of 16 match and the English team went on to lose to Real Madrid.<ref>{{cite news|title=Turkish referee Cuneyt Cakir's history of sending off England-based players in big matches|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/9912050/Turkish-referee-Cuneyt-Cakirs-history-of-sending-off-England-based-players-in-big-matches.html|access-date=13 April 2016|work=The Daily Telegraph|date=6 March 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160426234655/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/9912050/Turkish-referee-Cuneyt-Cakirs-history-of-sending-off-England-based-players-in-big-matches.html|archive-date=26 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Manager [[Alex Ferguson]] was so enraged by the decision that he refused to speak to the media after the game.<ref>{{cite news|title=Manchester United v Real Madrid: Sir Alex Ferguson too angry with referee Cuneyt Cakir to speak after defeat|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/champions-league/9910668/Manchester-United-v-Real-Madrid-Sir-Alex-Ferguson-too-angry-with-referee-Cuneyt-Cakir-to-speak-after-defeat.html|access-date=13 April 2016|work=The Daily Telegraph|date=6 March 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160426233044/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/champions-league/9910668/Manchester-United-v-Real-Madrid-Sir-Alex-Ferguson-too-angry-with-referee-Cuneyt-Cakir-to-speak-after-defeat.html|archive-date=26 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> UEFA confirmed that no action would be taken against the referee, concluding that he made the correct judgement.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Nakrani|first1=Sachin|title=Nani red card: Uefa backs Champions League referee Cuneyt Cakir|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/mar/06/uefa-referee-cuneyt-cakir-nani|access-date=13 April 2016|work=The Guardian|date=6 March 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160422115701/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/mar/06/uefa-referee-cuneyt-cakir-nani|archive-date=22 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Team selection===
Juventus defender [[Giorgio Chiellini]] was ruled out of the final with a calf injury picked up in training on 3 June.<ref>{{cite web |title=Giorgio Chiellini out of Champions League final against Barcelona |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jun/04/giorgio-chiellini-champions-league-final-juventus-barcelona |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=4 June 2015 |access-date=5 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150605044153/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jun/04/giorgio-chiellini-champions-league-final-juventus-barcelona |archive-date=5 June 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref> Barcelona had no injury concerns before the final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Juventus v Barcelona final: team news |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2253464.html |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=4 June 2015 |access-date=5 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150621214450/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2253464.html |archive-date=21 June 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Barcelona's [[Luis Suárez (Uruguayan footballer)|Luis Suárez]] had previously been involved in two controversies with players in the Juventus squad: in 2012, he was found guilty by an FA commission of using racially insulting language towards [[Patrice Evra]] in a game between their respective former clubs [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] and Manchester United, and at the [[2014 FIFA World Cup]] he escaped punishment for biting Chiellini but was punished retrospectively. Evra stated that he would shake Suárez's hand before the game, after Suarez had refused to shake his hand in their last match.<ref>{{cite news |title=Juventus defender Patrice Evra says he'll shake Luis Suarez's hand at Champions League final |url=http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/12040/9875108/juventus-defender-patrice-evra-says-hell-shake-luis-suarezs-hand-at-champions-league-final |publisher=Sky Sports (BSkyB) |date=6 June 2015 |access-date=6 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150607145227/http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/12040/9875108/juventus-defender-patrice-evra-says-hell-shake-luis-suarezs-hand-at-champions-league-final |archive-date=7 June 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Summary===
[[File:2012 2013 - 06 Xavi Hernández.jpg|thumb|left|upright|[[Xavi (footballer, born 1980)|Xavi]] came on as a substitute for the last of his 767 Barcelona appearances.]]

In the second minute, Barcelona's [[Javier Mascherano]] conceded a corner kick, which Juventus aimed at Evra but Barcelona cleared it. However two minutes later, [[Jordi Alba]] made a run on Barcelona's left, passing to [[Neymar]] and then [[Andrés Iniesta]] who set up [[Ivan Rakitić]] to score the first goal from close range. Rakitić's goal was Barcelona's fastest goal in a Champions League final,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.marca.com/en/2015/06/06/en/football/barcelona/1433622125.html|title=Rakitic scores Barça's fastest goal in Champions League final|date=6 June 2015|access-date=22 June 2015|publisher=Marca|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150622204409/http://www.marca.com/en/2015/06/06/en/football/barcelona/1433622125.html|archive-date=22 June 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> and the fourth fastest in a Champions League final overall.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d8010f2fd94-325085b39367-1000--uefa-champions-league-final-facts-and-figures/|title=UEFA Champions League final facts and figures|date=7 June 2015|access-date=22 June 2015|publisher=[[UEFA]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150609223222/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2255059.html|archive-date=9 June 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> In the 11th minute, [[Arturo Vidal]] of Juventus received the first yellow card for fouling [[Sergio Busquets]]. At half time, Barcelona led 1–0.<ref name=guardian>{{cite news|last1=Murray|first1=Scott|title=Juventus 1–3 Barcelona|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2015/jun/06/juventus-v-barcelona-champions-league-final-2015-live|access-date=6 June 2015|work=[[The Guardian]]|date=6 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160326193226/http://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2015/jun/06/juventus-v-barcelona-champions-league-final-2015-live|archive-date=26 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

Ten minutes into the second half, Juventus equalised: [[Claudio Marchisio]] back-heeled the ball to right-back [[Stephan Lichtsteiner]], who set up [[Carlos Tevez]]. Barcelona goalkeeper [[Marc-André ter Stegen]] blocked the shot from Tevez, but [[Álvaro Morata]] levelled the scores by putting the rebound into the net. In the 67th minute, Juventus appealed for a penalty when [[Paul Pogba]] went down in the area when challenged by [[Dani Alves]], but the referee did not give it. Two minutes later, Barcelona took the lead again when [[Lionel Messi]] shot from the edge of the area, [[Gianluigi Buffon]] blocked it and Suárez put in the rebound. Soon after, Neymar put the ball into the net from Alba's cross, but it was disallowed as the referee deemed that Neymar had headed it into his own hand.<ref name=guardian/>

In the 78th minute, Barcelona substituted Iniesta, who gave his captain's armband to [[Xavi (footballer, born 1980)|Xavi]], making his 767th and final appearance for Barcelona.<ref name=xavi>{{cite news|last1=Corrigan|first1=Dermot|title=Xavi Hernandez says he's happy but 'nostalgic' after final Barcelona game|url=http://www.espnfc.co.uk/barcelona/story/2481745/xavi-hernandez-happy-but-nostalgic-after-final-barca-game|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=ESPN|date=6 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160422162949/http://www.espnfc.co.uk/barcelona/story/2481745/xavi-hernandez-happy-but-nostalgic-after-final-barca-game|archive-date=22 April 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Soon after, Juventus made three substitutions in quick succession: Vidal was replaced with [[Roberto Pereyra]] and Morata with [[Fernando Llorente]], while Evra made way for [[Kingsley Coman]]. In added time, Barcelona made their final two changes, as Rakitić made way for [[Jérémy Mathieu]] and the limping Suárez for [[Pedro (footballer, born 1987)|Pedro]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/champions-league/11652515/Champions-League-final-Juventus-vs-Barcelona-live.html|title=Barcelona crush Juventus in Champions League final: as it happened|date=6 June 2015|work=Daily Telegraph|access-date=8 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150607180201/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/champions-league/11652515/Champions-League-final-Juventus-vs-Barcelona-live.html|archive-date=7 June 2015|url-status=live}}</ref>
In the final added minute, Alves handled the ball near the halfway line, and Juventus launched the resulting free kick towards the Barcelona goal, where it was cleared. The ensuing counter-attack ended with Pedro giving an assist to Neymar, who scored the final kick to make it 3–1.<ref name=guardian/>

