==Post-match==
[[File:Neymar_(16245492137).jpg|thumb|left|[[Neymar]]'s tenth goal of the season in the final meant that three players shared the honour of top scorer.]]
With the win, Barcelona became the first European club to achieve the [[treble (association football)#Continental trebles|treble]] twice.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d801f260f58-ddd88205fd28-1000--barcelona-break-new-ground-with-second-treble/|title=Barcelona break new ground with second treble|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=6 June 2015|access-date=30 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171119115620/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2251733.html|archive-date=19 November 2017|url-status=live}}</ref> Their fifth European title put them joint third with [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], until Liverpool won the [[2019 UEFA Champions League final|2019 final]] to achieve their sixth title. Bayern also went on to win their sixth trophy a year later, in the [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020 final]]. If only the Champions League era is considered, this is their fourth title, putting them one title behind [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]].<ref name="records">{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0cd2c6a4864d-29970ae1207c-1000--barcelona-bolster-pedigree-with-fifth-triumph/|title=Barcelona bolster pedigree with fifth triumph|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=6 June 2015|access-date=7 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150609010355/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2251945.html|archive-date=9 June 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> Madrid extended their lead by winning a fifth title in [[2016 UEFA Champions League final|2016]] and, as of [[2024 UEFA Champions League final|2024]], they have won nine titles in the Champions League era.
[[File:Col·leccions del Museu del FC Barcelona 27.jpg|thumb|235x235px|The UEFA Champions League trophy in [[FC Barcelona Museum]].]]
On the other hand, Juventus became the first team to lose six finals: they previously shared the record with [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] and [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]].<ref name="records"/> [[Patrice Evra]] became the first player to play in four losing UEFA Champions League finals, having previously been a runner-up in 2004 (with [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]]), 2009 and 2011 (both with Manchester United).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0222-0e910f1609e9-c7e3619f364b-1000--evra-first-to-lose-four-champions-league-finals/|title=Evra first to lose four Champions League finals|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=6 June 2015|access-date=7 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150608200033/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2254098.html|archive-date=8 June 2015|url-status=live}}</ref>

Neymar's goal meant that three players shared the title of top scorer in the competition, alongside Messi and Cristiano Ronaldo. He became the first player since [[Kaká]] in [[2006–07 UEFA Champions League|2006–07]] to be top scorer apart from those two. Messi was the top assister with six, with Ronaldo among the runners-up with four. After the final, both Messi and Ronaldo had a record 77 Champions League goals, but Ronaldo had one more if qualifiers were to be included.<ref>{{cite news|title=Neymar, Messi and Ronaldo top scoring charts|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0cd2c6a4864d-6fcf927ecffc-1000--neymar-messi-and-ronaldo-top-scoring-charts/|access-date=13 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=6 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151205045913/http://www.uefa.com/news/newsid%3D2251883.html#three+joint+scorers|archive-date=5 December 2015|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Reactions===
[[File:2015 UEFA Super Cup 65.jpg|thumb|upright|Despite his victory, there was uncertainty whether Barcelona manager [[Luis Enrique]] would remain in his position.]]

Speaking to [[Canal+ (French TV channel)|Canal+]] after his final game for Barcelona, Xavi said he felt nostalgic knowing that he would never play for the team again, but deemed it "incredible" to win the Champions League in his last appearance. He admitted that Barcelona had been shaken by Juventus' equaliser, but had recovered through Messi doing "his thing". Xavi praised manager [[Luis Enrique]] for his successes despite his disputes with the club's board; there was uncertainty whether Luis Enrique would remain in his position for the following season.<ref name=xavi/>

Luis Enrique called the final "spectacular", and his team's opponents "absolutely top-level". Of the Juventus team, he singled out Buffon for praise. In his own side, he commented that Neymar had gone from "very, very good" in his previous season, to "spectacular" in this one, ranking him among the world's best players. Luis Enrique stated that Suárez' performances had been worth the money Barcelona paid for him, despite initial doubt whether he would adapt to a new team. In spite of Barcelona's treble, the manager concluded that it had been "a difficult year, a transitional year".<ref name=elated>{{cite news|last1=Haslam|first1=Andrew|title=Luis Enrique 'elated' after passing final test|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c510c2f94f8-e89bc1f52f4c-1000--luis-enrique-elated-after-passing-final-test/|access-date=13 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=7 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170515035544/http://www.uefa.com/news/newsid=2247964.html#luis+enrique+elated|archive-date=15 May 2017|url-status=live}}</ref>

Juventus manager [[Massimiliano Allegri]] praised his team for their efforts, and said that despite a few regrets, they could exit with confidence from their performance. He commented that the team reaching the final had inspired the city of [[Turin]] and the whole of Italy. Allegri added that to improve on the season would not mean winning a treble, but remaining within Europe's top 8 teams for the future.<ref name=elated/>

===Subsequent matches===
After winning the final, Barcelona qualified for the [[2015 UEFA Super Cup]] against compatriots [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], winners of the [[2014–15 UEFA Europa League]]. In the match on 11 August in [[Tbilisi]], Georgia, Barcelona won 5–4 with Pedro scoring an extra-time winner. It was their fifth win in the competition, equalling Milan's record.<ref>{{cite news|title=Pedro is Barcelona's UEFA Super Cup hero again|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c51114c505d-63dadcebc3cd-1000--pedro-is-barcelona-s-uefa-super-cup-hero-again/|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=UEFA|date=11 August 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160311125240/http://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/season=2015/matches/round=2000639/match=2015790/postmatch/report/index.html#pedro+barcas+hero|archive-date=11 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

In December, Barcelona travelled to Japan as Europe's representative in the [[2015 FIFA Club World Cup]]. They defeated China's [[Guangzhou Evergrande]] 3–0 in the semi-finals and [[CA River Plate|River Plate]] of Argentina by the same score in the [[2015 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]]. It was a record third win in the competition, and their fifth trophy of the calendar year.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Begley|first1=Emlyn|title=River Plate 0-3 Barcelona|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35141742|access-date=11 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=20 December 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180221230404/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35141742|archive-date=21 February 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

